+FIXED
+
+# cluster ci-b71ed146fd535ea5 - CI regression: build / linux-x86_64
+
+## Verdict
+
+FIXED. The finding is REAL on this branch at the pinned train commit
+`fa4a5f2e785c73e32216931e33cdbe140c06647d`: the hosted `build / linux-x86_64` job
+(run 43786984969, job 133036727661) stopped compiling the test target
+`MixerSendsOnFadersTest`, not the product:
+
+```
+tests/src/gui/MixerSendsOnFadersTest.cpp:278:81: error: no matching function for call to
+    'QStringList::QStringList(const int&, QString)'
+  note: candidate template ignored: deduced conflicting types for parameter
+        'InputIterator' ('int' and 'QString')
+```
+
+The job builds with Qt 5.15 (`/usr/include/x86_64-linux-gnu/qt5/QtCore/qstringlist.h`).
+There `QStringList` is its own class and its only two-argument constructor is the
+inherited iterator-pair template; `QStringList(count, value)` is a Qt 6
+`QList<QString>` constructor (`QList(qsizetype size, parameter_type value)`). Qt 6
+aliases `QStringList` to `QList<QString>`, so every Qt 6 host here compiled the same line.
+This is a test-source portability regression, not product behaviour and not runner
+infrastructure. It was introduced by `97d786c18e` (the new
+`theFaderAnswerNamesWhatEveryFaderDrives` case).
+
+The local Qt 5.15.19 tree reproduces the hosted compiler input exactly. Recompiling that
+translation unit against Qt 5 through the recorded compile command produced:
+
+```
+tests/src/gui/MixerSendsOnFadersTest.cpp:278:81: error: no matching function for call to
+    'QStringList::QStringList(const int&, QString)'
+  note: candidate template ignored: deduced conflicting types for parameter
+        'InputIterator' ('int' and 'QString')
+COMPILE_RC=1
+```
+
+## Cause and fix
+
+`tests/src/gui/MixerSendsOnFadersTest.cpp:278` (before the fix) built the expected fader
+list with `QStringList(channels, QStringLiteral("volume"))`. The assertion itself is
+correct and is preserved exactly: the list must have one `"volume"` entry per mixer
+channel. Only the construction is replaced with the portable spelling:
+
+```cpp
+QStringList expectedFaders;
+expectedFaders.reserve(channels);
+for (int index = 0; index < channels; ++index) { expectedFaders.append(QStringLiteral("volume")); }
+QVERIFY(faders == expectedFaders);
+```
+
+`reserve()` and `append()` are declared by both Qt 5's `QStringList`/`QList` and Qt 6's
+`QList<QString>`. A scan of every first-party C/C++ file found this as the only
+two-argument `QStringList(...)` construction; the neighbouring `QStringList({...})` and
+one-argument forms in `MixerPinnedStripsTest.cpp` and `ClipWarpPersistenceTest.cpp` are
+not affected.
+
+## Regression test
+
+New `tests/Qt5QStringListSizeCheck.py`, registered as ctest
+`Qt5QStringListSizeCheck` in `tests/CMakeLists.txt:9334` and in
+`tests/fork-sources.txt:852`. It has two deterministic halves:
+
+1. A source scan of first-party C/C++ for a two-argument `QStringList(...)` construction
+   that is not an iterator pair. Its own red fixture is the failing
+   `QStringList(count, value)` form; its green fixture is the append loop.
+2. Where `pkg-config` finds Qt 5, it regenerates the Qt 5 `.moc` for
+   `tests/src/gui/MixerSendsOnFadersTest.cpp` and recompiles that real translation unit
+   `-fsyntax-only` through `build/compile_commands.json` with the Qt 5 headers, i.e. the
+   hosted job's compiler input. The bad and good fixtures are compiled first and must
+   respectively fail and pass on that Qt 5 tree. A scan finding whose translation unit
+   compiles clean is dropped as a false positive; a host without Qt 5 reports the probe
+   as skipped, never as passed.
+
+## Verification (all commands unpiped; logs in the cluster LOG FOLDER)
+
+| Step | Command | Exit | Log |
+| --- | --- | --- | --- |
+| Qt 5 reproduction of the hosted input | retargeted `-fsyntax-only` of `MixerSendsOnFadersTest.cpp` | 1 (compile) | `02-qt5-actual-repro.log` |
+| RED, fix reverted | `python3 tests/Qt5QStringListSizeCheck.py --root . --build-dir build` | 1 | `21-check-reverted.log` |
+| Qt 6 target with the bug present | `cmake --build build --target MixerSendsOnFadersTest -j2` | 0 | `20-build-reverted.log` |
+| restore the fix | `git apply <LOGS>/fix.patch` | 0 | `fix.patch` |
+| GREEN build | `cmake --build build --target MixerSendsOnFadersTest -j2` | 0 | `22-build-restored.log` |
+| GREEN check | `python3 tests/Qt5QStringListSizeCheck.py --root . --build-dir build` | 0 | `23-check-restored.log` |
+| ctest form | `cd build/tests && QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen ctest -R '^Qt5QStringListSizeCheck$' --output-on-failure` | 0 | `35-ctest-final.log` |
+| affected family | `cd build/tests && QT_QPA_PLATFORM=offscreen ctest -R '^(MixerSendsOnFadersTest\|Qt5QStringListSizeCheck\|MixerPinnedStripsTest\|Qt5QListApiCheck\|Qt5QStringListAssignmentCheck\|Qt5QStringListBraceCheck\|Qt5QStringListAssignCheck)$' --output-on-failure` | 0 (7/7 passed) | `25-ctest-family-final.log` |
+| gates | `python3 ~/.local/share/zene-bunny-fix/qwen/feature_check.py <worktree>` | 0 | `36-feature-check-final2.log` |
+
+The Qt 6 target building while the Qt 5 check fails is the point of the regression: the
+Qt 6 hosts never saw this defect. `fix.patch` contains only the test-source hunk because
+the defect is in test source; reversing it is the product-change equivalent for this
+finding (there is no `src/`, `include/` or `plugins/` hunk to reverse).
+
+## Files changed
+
+* `tests/src/gui/MixerSendsOnFadersTest.cpp:278-282` - portable expected-list construction, same exact-list assertion.
+* `tests/Qt5QStringListSizeCheck.py` - new deterministic Qt 5 source/compile regression.
+* `tests/CMakeLists.txt:9322-9339` - registers `Qt5QStringListSizeCheck`.
+* `tests/fork-sources.txt:846-855` - registers the new checker and this report.
+* `tests/upstream-modifications.txt:5991` - declares the `tests/CMakeLists.txt` registration; the `MixerSendsOnFadersTest.cpp` ledger entry now records this cluster too.
+* `docs/qwen-fixes-2026-10/cluster-ci-b71ed146fd535ea5.md` - this report.
+
+## Self-check
+
+* The regression test FAILS with the fix reverted and PASSES with it. Final RED:
+  `git apply -R <LOGS>/fix.patch` (exit 0), then
+  `python3 tests/Qt5QStringListSizeCheck.py --root . --build-dir build` exits **1**, with
+  one finding and the Qt 5 compile error quoted above (`21-check-reverted.log`). Final
+  GREEN: `git apply <LOGS>/fix.patch` (exit 0), the same checker exits **0**
+  (`23-check-restored.log`). The ctest form also exits **0** (`35-ctest-final.log`).
+* The test is deterministic: no wall-clock timing, sleeps or races. It reads checked-in
+  sources and the recorded compile command, retargets the Qt 6 include directories at the
+  pkg-config Qt 5 tree, generates the Qt 5 moc in a temporary directory, and compiles
+  `-fsyntax-only`. The only environment seam is the Qt 5 development tree; its absence is
+  reported as `probe: skipped`, not as a pass. The self-test fixtures run before the scan.
+* The fix is at the cause and complete: the expected list is built by the same
+  `reserve`/`append` primitives on both Qt generations, and the scan found no other
+  two-argument `QStringList(...)` construction in first-party sources. The assertion is
+  unchanged: it still compares the full list, one entry per mixer channel.
+* No collateral damage: only a test's expected-list construction changed. No product
+  source, audio path, undo state, command, project file or saved-data format was touched,
+  and no limit or check was added. The Qt 6 `MixerSendsOnFadersTest` still passes, as do
+  the neighbouring mixer and Qt 5 portability checks (7/7 above).
+* Not an ALREADY-FIXED or FALSE-POSITIVE verdict: the exact hosted line is present at
+  `tests/src/gui/MixerSendsOnFadersTest.cpp:278` on this branch and was reproduced with
+  the local Qt 5.15.19 headers before the fix.
+* Realtime: nothing in this change runs on the audio thread; it is test source and a
+  Python checker.
+* Undo/commands: no command, undo step, reversibility class or snapshot canary is
+  involved.
+* Independent QA: the reproduction is the two-command reversal above (`fix.patch`
+  reverse/apply, exit 1 then 0), and the checker is the entry point for the independent
+  verifier. No second agent ran it in this session, so this report does not claim an
+  independent result.
